 "narrative": "The new validation for H3 does not support acceptance. The original discovery suggested EGFR amplification may be associated with stronger EGFR dependency (signed effect +0.025; raw difference would be more negative), and its lower bound was very close to zero. However, the independent validation estimate is opposite in sign and essentially null (+/- adjusted interval -0.050 to +0.038). Because the validation interval is adjusted and broad, it neither confirms a clinically meaningful positive effect nor establishes a strong negative effect. The combined evidence therefore remains too ambiguous to accept or reject H3, but the most likely interpretation is that any overall EGFR-amplification effect is small, unstable, or context-dependent. I keep H3 active because H14 can test whether the effect differs by KRAS mutation status, which is biologically plausible given mutual exclusivity and pathway bypass between EGFR and KRAS alterations.\n\nThe reverse NFE2L2 claim H13 should now be accepted. The direct evidence is extraordinarily strong and precise: signed estimate +0.796 with a 95% interval +0.776 to +0.816, far beyond the approximate 10% relative threshold. In raw biological terms, KEAP1-loss cells appear to have much weaker NFE2L2 dependency than KEAP1-intact cells, which is the opposite of the initially anticipated direction but is exactly what the data show. This is a dataset-specific association; it does not prove that KEAP1 loss causes reduced NFE2L2 dependency, nor that it will generalize to other cell lines or screening platforms. I keep H13 active so that it can be considered for independent validation, given that the original anticipated direction failed and the magnitude is biologically surprising.\n\nOverall, the strongest supported findings in this supplied dataset remain: MSI-high cells have much stronger WRN dependency, TP53-loss cells have much weaker USP7 dependency, and KEAP1-loss cells have much weaker NFE2L2 dependency. Negative or not-supported findings include KRAS-mutation effects on RIT1, BRAF-V600E effects on BRAF dependency, ARID1A-loss effects on SMARCA2 dependency, and MYC-amplification effects on POLR2A dependency. EGFR amplification, KRAS dependency by KRAS mutation, ERBB2 amplification, TP53-loss effects on MDM2 dependency, and serum effects on STAT3 dependency remain uncertain. The most informative next analyses are to run H13 if validation can be requested, then the registered follow-up interactions H14-H19 and the KRAS adenocarcinoma subgroup H16."
