RBB2 amplification, RIT1 by KRAS status, MYC amplification effects on POLR2A/DHX9/RPL11, RB1 loss effects on KIF18A, BRCA2 loss effects on WRN, CDKN2A loss effects on USP7, and TMED10 specificity probes for TP53/SMARCA4 loss all appear null or far below the meaningful threshold. KRAS mutation overall (H1), KRAS subgroup/interaction contrasts, serum effects on STAT3, and most small accepted-effect interactions are not accepted; some are deferred because intervals are too broad or the effect magnitude is too small to be scientifically important. The remaining meaningful uncertainty is mostly context modification rather than the main effects: MTAP-loss interaction for USP7 (H18), high-Cas9 interaction for USP7 (H23), IFN-gamma/serum interaction for STAT3 (H25), and several untested broad probes such as TMED10/POLR2A/STAT3 predictors.\n\nNext, the most informative single analysis is independent validation of H20. If H20 validates, the study would have four strong, dataset-specific dependency associations supported either by held-out validation or by extremely large internally replicated discovery/subgroup effects. If it does not validate, the large H20 result would need to be reinterpreted despite strong within-data consistency. After H20 validation, continue with untested specificity/outcome probes and a small number of higher-value interactions, while treating all conclusions as associations in this supplied dataset rather than causal mechanisms or externally generalizable claims."
