servations.  H69 is the most interesting non-accepted result. The registered claim was that CDKN2A loss would produce weaker MDM2 dependency among TP53-intact cells, direction +1. The signed estimate was -0.119 (95% -0.158 to -0.079), i.e., the opposite direction: CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. I reject the original H69 direction because the evidence is opposite and the interval excludes the originally claimed weaker-dependency direction. I do not immediately accept the reversed association: the point estimate is only moderately above the 0.1 benchmark, the interval lower bound is 0.079, and there is substantial multiplicity plus possible confounding because 9p21 CDKN2A deletions can co-occur with MTAP loss and broader 9p alterations. I therefore registered a linked reversed proposal for CDKN2A loss as a predictor of stronger MDM2 dependency in TP53-intact cells, with initial status unresolved for later evidence review.  H70 tests STK11 loss as a predictor of stronger KRAS dependency: signed -0.002 (95% -0.022 to +0.018), essentially null; I reject and close H70. H71 tests NF1 loss as a predictor of stronger KRAS dependency: signed -0.020 (95% -0.049 to +0.009). This is small and opposite to the anticipated stronger-dependency direction; I reject and close H71. H74 tests MSI-high as a predictor of stronger DHX9 dependency: signed +0.017 (95% -0.017 to +0.052). This is small and well below the benchmark, supporting outcome specificity of the accepted MSI-high WRN association; I reject and close H74. H75 tests SMARCA4 loss as a predictor of stronger POLR2A dependency: signed -0.005 (95% -0.024 to +0.014). This supports outcome specificity of the accepted SMARCA2 association; I reject and close H75. H76 tests residual ARID1A-loss effects on SMARCA2 dependency within SMARCA4-loss cells: signed +0.034 (95% -0.013 to +0.082). The upper bound is below the 0.1 benchmark and the interval includes zero, so there is no strong evidence for additional ARID1A co-dependency beyond the dominant SMARCA4-loss effect; I reject and close H76.  The highest-priority validation request is H20. H20 is accepted, huge in discovery, internally robust in ARID1A-loss, ARID1A-intact, and Avana-library subsets, but not yet independently validated. Validating it is the most informative use of the one requested validation this iteration.",
