d as an ambiguous possible broader CDKN2A-MDM2 association, and I requested validation for it because it is the best new candidate for a moderate secondary axis rather than a small null.\n\nH96/R82 tested whether the TP53-intact CDKN2A-MDM2 association is modified by lineage subtype. The interaction was signed -0.058 (95% -0.148 to +0.031), opposite to the registered direction and below the importance benchmark. There is no evidence that adenocarcinoma status meaningfully modifies the accepted TP53-intact effect, so H96 is rejected and closed. H97/R83 tested specificity by asking whether CDKN2A loss also predicts weaker USP7 dependency in TP53-intact cells. The estimate was essentially null: signed -0.003 (95% -0.025 to +0.019). This supports the interpretation that CDKN2A loss is not a broad p53-axis marker with generic USP7 effects, and H97 is rejected and closed.\n\nThe context probes for the accepted large axes were negative. H98/R84 found no evidence that higher aneuploidy modifies the MSI-high association with stronger WRN dependency: signed +0.022 (95% -0.037 to +0.081). H99/R85 found no evidence that PTEN loss modifies the TP53-loss association with weaker USP7 dependency: signed +0.064 (95% -0.145 to +0.273). Both are rejected and closed. These nulls are reassuring for the robustness of H10 and H11 across these contexts, but they should not be interpreted as equivalence tests.\n\nThe new mechanistic probes were mostly null or inconsistent. TP53 loss did not support weaker RPL11 dependency (H100/R86: +0.004; 95% -0.016 to +0.024) or stronger WRN dependency (H101/R87: -0.005; 95% -0.034 to +0.023). EGFR amplification did not support stronger STAT3 dependency (H102/R88: -0.034; 95% -0.068 to +0.001), and ERBB2 amplification did not support stronger STAT3 or RIT1 dependency at a meaningful size (H103/R89: +0.022; 95% -0.015 to +0.059; H104/R90: -0.006; 95% -0.035 to +0.023). MYC amplification in TP53-intact cells also did not support stronger MDM2 dependency (H105/R91: +0.008; 95% -0.023 to +0.040). All are rejected and closed as not supporting the registered associations.\n\nTo allocate follow-up efficiently, I registered one proposal for a broader CDKN2A-MDM2 test without the TP53-loss restriction. This will synthesize the current pattern if H95 is validated: CDKN2A loss may mark stronger MDM2 dependency in both TP53 contexts, but the TP53-intact claim H77 remains the currently accepted axis. If H95 fails validation, the TP53-intact interpretation of H77 remains intact and the broader claim should be interpreted cautiously.",
