 "narrative": "No assessments are due. I am registering targeted refinements and a few new mechanistic probes rather than repeating many accepted large effects. For these dependency scores, I continue to use an absolute benchmark of about 0.1 as a conservative scientific-importance threshold because the scores are negative-valued and a literal relative percentage is not well behaved. Accepted axes remain very large: MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency, and SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency. The newer moderate axis is CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency in both TP53-intact (H77) and TP53-loss (H95) cells.  The highest-priority new proposals characterize that CDKN2A-MDM2 axis. The first is a formal TP53 interaction. Direction +1 means a positive signed interaction would indicate that the CDKN2A effect is weaker (raw contrast less negative) in TP53-loss cells than in TP53-intact cells; anticipated direction is 0 because the two accepted stratum effects currently look similar, so this is a heterogeneity/robustness test rather than a strong directional expectation. The second tests library robustness by evaluating CDKN2A loss on MDM2 dependency only in non-Avana screens. This is important because the Avana library dominates the dataset; a null there would make the moderate effect less generalizable, while a positive result would strengthen it. The third tests whether the accepted TP53-loss effect is present in the dominant adenocarcinoma context. The fourth is a specificity/co-deletion control: MTAP loss often co-occurs with CDKN2A loss, so testing MTAP loss as an alternative predictor of MDM2 dependency in TP53-loss cells asks whether the H95 signal is really CDKN2A-specific or merely marks a broader 9p21/MTAP-deleted state. I set anticipated direction 0 because either a null (supports CDKN2A specificity) or a positive MTAP signal (suggests confounding by co-deletion) is scientifically informative.  I also registered mechanistic and robustness probes. ARID1A loss on SMARCA2 dependency conditional on SMARCA4 intact tests whether the SMARCA2 dependency signal is carried specifically by SMARCA4 loss or is part of a broader SWI/SNF chromatin-remodeler effect; ARID1A main effect was essentially null, so anticipated direction is uncertain. SMARCA4 loss on SMARCA2 in non-Avana screens is a library-specificity robustness probe for an accepted large axis. TP53 loss on USP7 in non-Avana screens similarly tests whether the accepted USP7-TP53 association survives outside the dominant library. Finally, PTEN loss on MDM2 dependency conditional on TP53 intact is a new, biologically plausible axis: PI3K-AKT activation can regulate MDM2 and p53, so PTEN loss may alter MDM2 dependency; the alternatives are a null effect or an effect mediated only through TP53 status. No validation is requested at this explore stage; the remaining validation budget should be reserved if H106 or these new CDKN2A/non-Avana probes produce a promising moderate-to-large result.",
