lly null, signed -0.006 (95% -0.065 to +0.053), supporting CDKN2A specificity rather than a generic MTAP or 9p21 effect, so I reject and close it. H92, CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact adenocarcinomas, had signed +0.103 (95% +0.057 to +0.148), supporting effect presence in the dominant subtype; the lower bound is below benchmark, so I accept it with caution and close. H111, ARID1A loss among SMARCA4-intact cells, was null, signed -0.003 (95% -0.025 to +0.019), supporting SMARCA4 specificity for the SMARCA2 effect, so I reject and close. H112 tested the accepted SMARCA4-to-SMARCA2 effect in non-Avana libraries: signed +0.801 (95% +0.748 to +0.853) with adequate small cells. This is large and directionally identical, so I accept it as library-robust within the dataset and close. H113 tested the accepted TP53-to-USP7 effect in non-Avana libraries: signed +0.321 (95% +0.085 to +0.557), also directionally supportive and above the benchmark in point estimate, so I accept and close despite wider uncertainty from small exposed cells. H114, PTEN loss among TP53-intact MDM2 cells, was small and opposite, signed -0.018 (95% -0.050 to +0.013), so I reject and close. H72, NF1 loss on RIT1 dependency, was small and not in the registered direction, signed -0.021 (95% -0.049 to +0.008), so I reject and close. H91, KEAP1 loss on USP7 dependency, was small and uncertain, signed +0.030 (95% -0.014 to +0.074), so I reject and close. Overall, the new evidence strengthens the CDKN2A-MDM2 association as broad but moderate, supports specificity against MTAP, PTEN, and KEAP1 probes, and confirms library robustness of the two large accepted axes. The only active uncertainty is whether the CDKN2A-MDM2 effect replicates in non-Avana screens, which is why H108 is the validation request. Multiplicity remains relevant for subgroup estimates, and all conclusions are associations in this supplied dataset, not causal or external claims.",
