. The exposed cell count is small, and the interval is wide, so the precise effect size remains uncertain. However, because the main unconditional CDKN2A-MDM2 association H106 is accepted and the TP53-intact and TP53-loss strata H77 and H95 were independently validated, this non-Avana replication is sufficient to accept H108 as library-robust evidence within the supplied dataset. I therefore accept H108 and close it, while explicitly retaining uncertainty in its exact magnitude.  H67 new validation: The claim was that TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency specifically in MTAP-loss-positive cells. Discovery R57 was +0.284 with interval +0.105 to +0.463, which originally supported the claim. Independent validation R106 was +0.265, a similar and meaningful point estimate, but its independent interval was -0.047 to +0.576 and crossed zero. Thus the validation is directionally consistent but not statistically conclusive, especially with only 61 exposed cells. I do not reject H67 because there is no evidence against the direction and the point estimate remains plausible, but I also do not keep it accepted because the newly delivered independent evidence does not confirm a scientifically meaningful stratum-specific effect. I therefore downgrade H67 to unresolved and defer it. The broad TP53-loss effect on USP7 remains strongly supported by H11 and its validation R52, and the MTAP-intact stratum H68 remains supported by discovery. The interaction test H18 did not support meaningful modification of the TP53-USP7 association by MTAP status. Therefore, the most defensible conclusion is that TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency broadly, with the MTAP-positive subgroup currently uncertain because of imprecision.  Overall supported findings. Four large dataset-specific associations remain strongly supported: MSI-high versus MSI-low is associated with much stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss is associated with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency, and SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency. These effects are far above the 0.1 absolute benchmark and have independent validation or strong replicated context evidence. A new moderate axis is now supported: CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. This is accepted unconditionally H106, in TP53-intact cells H77, in TP53-loss cells H95, in the dominant adenocarcinoma lineage contexts H92 and H109, and now also in non-Avana screens H108. The point estimates for this axis are generally around 0.10 to 0.17, meaning the magnitude is scientifically meaningful but not as precise as the large axes. MTAP loss, PTEN loss, MYC amplification, and ARID1A-loss/SWI-SNF controls did not reproduce or modify this association in ways that suggest it is a generic co-deletion or broad p53-axis artifact.  Negative and specificity findings. The many negative tests continue to narrow the interpretation. KRAS mutation was not associated with stronger dependency_KRAS or dependency_RIT1; EGFR amplification, ERBB2 amplification, BRAF V600E, PIK3CA mutation, NF1 loss, RB1 loss, MYC amplification, BRCA2 loss, MTAP loss, PTEN loss, and ARID1A loss were generally not associated with the tested dependency outcomes at a meaningful size or direction. Cross-pathway probes such as KEAP1 loss on USP7 and TP53 loss on RPL11 or WRN were null or below benchmark. Context probes for the large axes, including aneuploidy modification of MSI-WRN, PTEN modification of TP53-USP7, and MTAP modification of TP53-USP7, did not show meaningful modification. These negative results support specificity of the accepted associations but do not constitute proof of absence or equivalence.  Unresolved uncertainty. The main current uncertainty in the newly synthesized CDKN2A-MDM2 axis is precision: several lower confidence bounds fall below the 0.1 benchmark even when the point estimates exceed it, especially in non-Avana and smaller TP53-loss cells. The MTAP-positive TP53-USP7 stratum is also unresolved after inconclusive validation. Several deferred exploratory axes remain active or deferred, but they are less decision-relevant than refining the accepted CDKN2A-MDM2 and TP53-USP7/MTAP questions.  Most informative next analysis. The next best use of analysis budget is H93, CDKN2A loss on MDM2 dependency among TP53-intact Avana-library cells. It directly addresses whether the accepted moderate CDKN2A-MDM2 axis holds in the largest and most precise library context and in the strongest TP53-intact context. A positive result would make the CDKN2A-MDM2 conclusion more complete; a null or underpowered result would indicate that the current support is adequate but context-specific and should be described with stronger caveats. Secondary low-priority follow-ups include H73, H90, and other deferred pathway probes, but these should not displace H93 unless H93 closes the CDKN2A-MDM2 context uncertainty. All conclusions remain associations in this supplied dataset, not causal claims or claims of external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Round-8 synthesis. Due assessments handled. H77 kept accepted/closed after validation R78: CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 in TP53-intact cells, moderate effect. H66 kept reject/closed: MTAP loss not associated with USP7. H108 accepted/closed after validation R105: CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 in non-Avana screens; point estimate moderate and interval excludes zero, but lower bound below 0.1 and cells small. H67 downgraded from accept to unresolved/deferred after validation R106 crossed zero; MTAP-positive TP53-USP7 stratum direction consistent but underpowered/inconclusive. Broad TP53-USP7 H11 remains accepted, H68 remains accepted, H18 interaction rejected.  Consolidated final conclusions: Accepted large axes: H10 MSI-high -> stronger WRN; H13 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2; H11 TP53 loss -> weaker USP7; H20 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2. Accepted moderate axis: H106 unconditional CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2, with supporting accepted strata H77 TP53-intact, H95 TP53-loss, H92 TP53-intact adenocarcinoma, H109 TP53-loss adenocarcinoma, H108 non-Avana. Library robustness for large axes accepted via H112 and H113. Negative probes narrow specificity. Next priority: analyze H93 if not closed, to complete Avana TP53-intact CDKN2A context. Keep H67 deferred unless larger evidence or refined subgroup analysis becomes useful. Continue using 0.1 absolute dependency-score benchmark due to negative continuous scores."
