  "narrative":
    "I assessed all newly delivered discovery estimates using the same signed-estimate convention and the practical absolute benchmark of about 0.1 on the dependency-score scale. Dependency scores are negative-valued, so a literal relative 10% change is unstable near zero; the 0.1 benchmark is therefore used as a conservative scientific-importance threshold rather than a causal claim. For direction -1 mean differences, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency in the exposed group. For interactions, a positive signed estimate represents a larger focal-subgroup association when the hypothesis was registered that way. H93 tested CDKN2A loss on MDM2 dependency among TP53-intact Avana-library cells. The signed estimate was +0.117, 95% CI +0.074 to +0.160, with adequate cell sizes (281 exposed, 1121 comparator). The lower bound is slightly below the 0.1 benchmark, but the point estimate is above it and the result is highly coherent with the already accepted broad CDKN2A-MDM2 association and with independently validated TP53-intact and TP53-loss strata. I therefore accept H93 as a context-complete replication in the dominant TP53-intact Avana stratum and close it. H115 tested the accepted KEAP1-loss to weaker NFE2L2 association in non-Avana screens. The signed estimate was +0.794, 95% CI +0.746 to +0.842, with 40 exposed and 195 comparator cells. Although exposed cells are modest, the effect is enormous, far above the benchmark, and directionally identical to the broad validated effect. I accept it with a small-cell precision caveat and close it, supporting library robustness of this major axis within this dataset. H90 tested whether MSI-high remains associated with much stronger WRN dependency in cells with aneuploidy above the median. The signed estimate was +0.801, 95% CI +0.761 to +0.841, with 65 exposed cells. This is a large, directionally supportive context replication, so I accept and close H90. H116 is the most important new discovery: PTEN loss was associated with stronger STAT3 dependency, signed estimate +0.267, 95% CI +0.215 to +0.319, with 266 exposed cells. This is far above the practical benchmark and biologically plausible through PI3K/PTEN-related signaling pathways. However, it is a single new-axis discovery among several STAT3 probes, the estimate is unadjusted for co-occurring molecular alterations, and independent confirmation is not yet available. I therefore record H116 as unresolved but active and request independent validation rather than prematurely accepting it as a new supported axis. The remaining STAT3 probes did not support a coherent broader PI3K/RAS pathway effect: H117 STK11 loss was +0.015, CI -0.014 to +0.043; H118 KRAS mutation was -0.018, CI -0.042 to +0.007; H73 PIK3CA mutation was -0.014, CI -0.041 to +0.014. All are small, uncertain, and far below the benchmark, so I reject and close them. H125 tested Cas9-activity modification of the CDKN2A-MDM2 interaction. The signed interaction was +0.048, 95% CI -0.027 to +0.123. It does not support a meaningful high-Cas9 interaction, but the upper bound crosses the practical benchmark and the cell counts, while adequate, are not large. I keep H125 unresolved and deferred rather than rejecting it as proof of absence. H126 tested mutation-burden modification of the CDKN2A-MDM2 interaction and was essentially null, -0.001, CI -0.069 to +0.068, entirely below the benchmark; I reject and close it. H119 tested BRCA2 loss on DHX9 dependency and found a small null effect, +0.012, CI -0.020 to +0.044. H120 tested MSI-high on POLR2A dependency and found a small opposite/null effect, -0.004, CI -0.035 to +0.028. H122 tested SMARCA4 loss on KIF18A dependency and found a small opposite/null effect, -0.007, CI -0.026 to +0.013. All are below the benchmark and do not support new axes; I reject and close them. Overall, the new evidence strengthens the accepted large axes: MSI-high-WRN remains strong in high-aneuploidy cells, KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency is library-robust, and the CDKN2A-MDM2 association is now replicated in the largest TP53-intact Avana context. It also identifies a potentially major new PTEN-loss to stronger STAT3 dependency association, but I hold final acceptance pending independent validation. Multiplicity remains relevant for new subgroup or subgroup-marker claims, and all current conclusions remain associations in this supplied dataset rather than causal or externally generalizable effects.",
